GNU gzip for OpenVMS
====================

This is GNU gzip @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified release plus the patches in the vms-gzip project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-gzip

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU gzip is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The manual is GZIP.TXT;
GZIP.1 is the manual page.

What is installed
-----------------
    GZIP$ROOT:[BIN]GZIP.EXE
    GZIP$ROOT:[DOC]                   this file, COPYING, NEWS, the manual
    GZIP$ROOT:[000000]GZIP$SETUP.COM  defines the commands
    SYS$STARTUP:GZIP$STARTUP.COM      defines GZIP$ROOT

Setting up
----------
The kit installs GZIP$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define GZIP$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:GZIP$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the commands:

    $ @GZIP$ROOT:[000000]GZIP$SETUP.COM

Using it
--------
    $ gzip "-k" file.txt             ! file.txt.gz, keeping file.txt
    $ gunzip file.txt.gz             ! gzip -d
    $ zcat file.txt.gz               ! to SYS$OUTPUT
    $ gzip "-l" file.txt.gz          ! list it
    $ gzip "-r" [.dir]               ! every file in a directory tree

A name like file.txt.gz needs an ODS-5 disk.  gunzip also unpacks .Z
(compress), .z (pack) and .zip files with a single member.  The shell
scripts upstream installs beside gzip (zcmp, zdiff, zgrep, zless, zmore,
znew, zforce, gzexe) are not included.

Text and binary files
---------------------
A VMS text file (variable-length or VFC records) is compressed as text:
its records become lines ending in LF, as on Unix, so the compressed file
can be unpacked anywhere.  Decompressing gives a Stream_LF file with the
same lines.  Any other file (stream, fixed-length records: executables,
kits, binary data) is compressed byte for byte.

Exit status
-----------
Under DCL a failed run exits with an error-severity status, so ON ERROR and
IF .NOT. $STATUS work; the exit code N is ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8.  Exit
code 2 (a warning, such as trailing garbage ignored) has warning severity.
Under a GNV shell, $? is the exit code as on Unix.

Upper-case options and batch jobs
---------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL DCL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of
a command and the C run-time library passes them to the program in lower
case.  Batch jobs use TRADITIONAL even when interactive logins are set to
EXTENDED.  Quote options with capitals (such as "-S" and "-N"), use the
long forms, or start the procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED
